course: dictionary
lesson: 2096
title: What is context?
level: primary-5
stars: 2
explain: Context is the words and sentences around a word. They often give clues to its meaning, so you can work out a word you have not met before. «The arid desert had no water at all.» No water tells you that arid means very dry.
explain: Context also means the situation or background around an event. It helps explain why something happened. If a school closes early and the playground is full of snow, the snow is the context that explains why.
explain: Taking something out of context means taking it away from its surroundings, so its meaning changes or becomes unfair. A single quote can seem rude or silly when it is read on its own, but it may be fine when you read the words before and after it.
explain: Context clues are very useful, but they are not always enough. When you are still unsure, check a dictionary. Remember to test your meaning: swap it into the sentence and see if it still makes sense.
fact: Context means the words and sentences around a word.
fact: Context clues can help you work out the meaning of an unknown word.
fact: «In context» means understanding the time and place of something, especially in history.
fact: «Out of context» means taken away from its surroundings, so the meaning is changed or unfair.
fact: In a maths word problem, the context is the real-life story around the sum.
exercise: «The arid desert had no water at all.» What does arid mean? | Very noisy | Very wet | *Very dry | Very cold
exercise: What is the context of a word? | *The words and sentences around it that help you understand it | The number of letters in the word | The spelling of the word | The opposite of the word
exercise: What does «taken out of context» mean? | Written in capital letters | Taken to the shops for a trip | *Taken away from its surroundings, so the meaning is changed | Read aloud twice
test: In history, what does putting events «in context» help you understand? | Which day of the week it was | The colour of the page | *The time and place | How many people were there
test: In a maths word problem, what is the context? | The number of digits in the numbers | The sign for add or take away | The answer to the sum | *The real-life story around the sum
exam: Maya reads, «The sentence around a homophone, its context, tells you which spelling is needed.» What does the context help her decide? | How long the sentence is | Who wrote the book | The colour of the font | *Which spelling is needed
